<Base title="About — Paul Hendrie">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1 class="ignite">Paul Hendrie</h1>

    <p>I'm a learning designer working at the meeting point of three
    things: English for Academic Purposes, the craft of designing learning
    experiences, and — for the last few years — the use of AI to build
    kinds of support that used to be impossible for one person to make.</p>

    <p>My home discipline is in-sessional EAP: the academic English support
    that runs alongside students' degree courses rather than before them.
    It's a discipline that teaches you humility about language (academic
    English is nobody's first language), precision about support (scaffolds
    are built to come down), and respect for the disciplines students are
    actually trying to join. Everything else I make carries those habits.</p>

    <Note>
      Paul: two or three sentences of career specifics belong here —
      institutions, years, roles — at whatever level of detail you like.
      The paragraph above deliberately works without them.
    </Note>

    <p>These days most of my work involves AI somewhere in the loop: a
    memory palace for EAP practitioners, an academic writing course two
    hundred lessons deep, an app that teaches Legitimation Code Theory as
    a constellation, and an open collection of frameworks and skill files
    for designing learning with AI. The stance is the same across all of
    it — <a href="/approach/">the pedagogy decides, the model
    executes</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="ignite">Elsewhere</h2>
    <p>
      Email is best: <a href="mailto:paulhendrie@gmail.com">paulhendrie@gmail.com</a>.<br />
      Code and experiments live on <a href="https://github.com/henders23">GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <Note>
      No contact form. If you write, a person answers — that's the whole
      pipeline.
    </Note>
  </article>
